+    <section class="journal-section" aria-labelledby="journal-heading">
+      <div class="journal-header">
+        <h2 id="journal-heading">Roaster's Journal: From Canopy to Cup</h2>
+        <span class="journal-meta">Field Notes on Our Bean Sourcing Process</span>
+      </div>
+
+      <article id="journal-article" class="journal-article">
+        <p>
+          Every exceptional cup at The Daily Grind begins thousands of miles from our roastery, nestled in high-altitude micro-climates along the coffee belt. Over the past decade, we have transitioned away from commodity brokerage auctions in favor of direct-trade partnerships with smallholder family farms and cooperative washing stations in Ethiopia's Yirgacheffe region, Colombia's Huila highlands, and Guatemala's volcanic Antigua valley. By visiting these producers during harvest season each year, we build multi-year commitments that pay at least 40% above Fair Trade baseline prices, allowing growers to invest in shade-grown agroforestry and soil regeneration.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          Quality control starts on the mountainside, where cherries are selectively hand-picked only at peak crimson ripeness rather than strip-harvested. Within hours of picking, the fruit arrives at local wet mills to undergo carefully calibrated fermentation and processing. For our bright, floral single-origin offerings, cherries are depulped and fermented in spring-water tanks for 36 hours before drying slowly on raised African beds. For our sweeter espresso lots, we collaborate with growers on honey and natural processes that preserve mucilage sugars while preventing over-fermentation through hourly raking and moisture monitoring.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          Once dried to a stable 10.5% moisture content, parchment coffee rests in climate-controlled warehouses before hulling and rigorous Q-grader cupping. Only microlots scoring 86 points or higher on the Specialty Coffee Association scale are sealed in hermetic GrainPro bags to lock in volatile aromatics during ocean transit. When the green beans arrive at our local roastery, we conduct sample roasts to map each lot's density and moisture conductivity, tailoring custom roast curves that highlight inherent terroir—whether crisp bergamot acidity or deep caramelized cacao—without masking the farmer's craft behind excessive char.
+        </p>
+      </article>
+
